**Design idea.** Put the behaviour that is the same in a base class and make the behaviour that varies abstract, so each subclass supplies its own version. This is inheritance plus runtime polymorphism, and it follows the open/closed principle: adding a new kind of animal needs a new class, not edits to existing code.

    abstract class Animal {
        public String walk() { return "This animal walks on land"; }
        public abstract String eat();
    }

    class Herbivore extends Animal {
        @Override public String eat() { return "eats plants"; }
    }

    class Carnivore extends Animal {
        @Override public String eat() { return "eats animals"; }
    }

    class Omnivore extends Animal {
        @Override public String eat() { return "eats both plants and animals"; }
    }

    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            Animal[] zoo = { new Herbivore(), new Carnivore(), new Omnivore() };
            for (Animal a : zoo) {
                System.out.println(a.walk() + " and " + a.eat());
            }
        }
    }

**Why this works.** `walk()` is implemented once in `Animal`, so all kingdoms share the same sentence. `eat()` is abstract, so the compiler forces every subclass to define it, and the call `a.eat()` is resolved at runtime to the right version (dynamic dispatch).

**Alternatives and trade-offs.**
- Since the only variation is the diet text, a single class with a `Diet` enum or a list of food types would also work and avoids a class explosion; use inheritance when behaviour (not only data) differs.
- Use an interface (`Eater`, `Walker`) for capabilities that unrelated classes share, and composition (strategy pattern: an `EatingBehavior` object) if behaviours must change at runtime or combine in many ways.
- Do not duplicate `walk()` in each subclass.

Mention how you would test it: one assertion per kingdom's `eat()` output and one for the shared `walk()` output.
